let's start with the president  practicing the art of the deal even  while on vacation  he gave Democrats his bottom line for  any daca deal on amnesty for the illegal  immigrants called dreamers the president  tweeted today quote the Democrats have  been told and fully understand that  there can be no daca without the  desperately needed wall at the southern  border and an end to the horrible chain  migration and ridiculous lottery systems  of immigration etc we must protect our  country at all costs can the president  get the Democrats to guess and will his  own base accept amnesty let's discuss  that with Fox News contributor Steve  Cortez a former Trump campaign operative  and spokesman for the Hispanic 100 and  Steven wall Roy a law professor at the  University of Memphis and a former  federal prosecutor happy new year  gentlemen thank you for a year for  joining us this is going to be a  contentious issue as we turned it to the  March 5th deadline that is out there on  daca professor I want to start with you  you know my concern is that Democrats  they like the issue they just don't  actually want to solve it they always  say oh we need comprehensive we need to  do all these things the president's  asked is pretty simple let's build the  wall it's something that Democrats have  supported in the past is it unreasonable  to think that we should protect our  border and build a wall well it's not  unreasonable to think that we should  protect our border but we don't  necessarily need to build a wall in  order to do that I mean the actual wall  itself it were to cover the exact  complete expanse of the border would  cost tens of billions of dollars and  would probably not even be effective  because what sections of wall we already  have experience has shown that you can  tunnel under them or put ramps or  ladders over them so if you're going to  be spending tens of billions of dollars  on a wall that actually won't work and  we'll simply alienate relations with  Mexico why not use that money in a  better way by increasing border security  agents and doing economic developments  in Mexico to reduce the demand for  illegal immigration increased guest  worker programs there are a lot more  efficient efficacious ways of tackling  this problem that would  not alienate Mexico would actually work  and would be a better use of our tax  dollars well Steve Cortez the the  president's gonna get is his wall isn't  he he's gonna get the 1.8 billion  dollars that he wanted I don't know how  he could settle for anything less as  President Obama said elections have  consequences right and this is one of  the things the president promised he was  going to do  they sure do and then Jason this was a  pillar of his electoral mandate and I  called him president promise keeper  because what he did in 2017 is keep one  promise after another we are going to  get that wall and by the way the walls  not just good for the United States I  would disagree with the professor it's  also actually good for Mexico because it  has a problem on its southern border  largely because of our poorest southern  border so getting our border under  control between Texas and Arizona and  California and Mexico is good policies  often good good neighbors need good  fences and that's what we need between  the US and Mexico but more importantly I  really want to give the president so  much credit for what he's already done  even before the big beautiful wall goes  up illegal crossings have plunged under  this president why because of clear-eyed  rhetoric and better enforcement and  don't take my word for it by the way the  director of ice Tom Holman said that  this president has done more for border  security than the six previous  presidents combined so he is already  enhancing our economic and national  security by securing our borders now  professor you gotta admit that the  numbers are reflective on the reduction  of the border crossing but they're still  happening what do you attribute that the  lower a crossing rate to be other than  the election of Donald Trump it's  important to remember that actually  immigration border crossings were  actually on the decline four years prior  to the election of Donald Trump we  actually now have a negative net outflow  of immigration there are more people  leaving than coming in and that was the  case well before Donald Trump was  elected and as to whether he I gotta  tell you I was a chairman of a committee  in Congress and we held multiple  hearings i could not i don't i would  love to see those numbers where you  think that we were actually on the  decline with with president obama i mean  that  siloam seekers others that were coming  across this border were much higher than  they are today well you know the source  that I would use right now would be  politifact which actually did a recent  study a few months ago on this very  thing but as to the politics of it let's  also remember that President Trump had  previously agreed with Chuck Schumer  Schumer and Nancy Pelosi to give them  daca reform in exchange for beefed up  border security without the wall why  would it make sense for them to now  negotiate against themselves and go  backwards as part of the negotiations  between the White House and Capitol Hill  but professor you'd be okay if it's not  necessarily a physical wall but if it  went to border security I would probably  personally disagree with you that  economic development and northern Mexico  is I don't know why the Americans have  to pay for that but what else  so you'd be okay right professor if it  was not just the wall but border  security and definition of that we might  want to haggle about correct right well  I think that actually increased border  security and increased guest worker  program would be a more efficient use of  our tax dollars than actually building a  wall which I think will be way more  expensive than we expect because of the  land rights that we would have to buy  through eminent domain and the fact that  it's not really very effective so I  think it makes sense to talk about  border security and daca reform without  the wall so Steve Cortes what what else  is the president going to have to get  because there is a long laundry list the  lottery asylum reform there's daca what  what else do you think is imperative  you've been close to Donald Trump what  do you think is imperative now Jason and  here's what I think is on the way and  here's what I've told the president  personally and I disagree with some of  my colleagues on Team Trump people whom  I respect I do believe the daca  recipients and they're not dreamers by  the way I don't call them dreamers  Americans have dreams too and they're  not kids they are adults but I believe  dr. recipients are a different category  of illegal immigrant those who truly did  not choose to come here as because they  were brought here as children so I  believe they should be protected and  again I disagree with a lot of people I  respect on that issue but having said  that and this is what I've told the  president I believe what he will do is  we need things in return and the big  things are borders secured  more barricades more resources for the  border but also we can't just stop there  and end to chain migration chain  migration has been a disaster for the  United States it affronts our national  security it affronts our economic  security right now and I'm the son of an  immigrant and an Hispanic the majority  of immigrant headed households in the  United States receive some form of  welfare that's a tragedy that's terrible  policy and it's nothing like our parents  and grandparents and great-grandparents  faced when they came here when they  wanted nothing from the United States  other than an opportunity so we're not  going immigration right largely because  of chain migration that has to end so we  have to have a grand bargain which I do  believe is going to happen in in January  daca recipients protected and to chain  migration resources of the border  gentlemen thank you a Happy New Year  appreciate you joining us in this  conversation earlier on Fox News Senator  Lindsey Graham came on and said there  was no possible way that we're gonna do  daca by itself that that was just  political theater it'll be interesting  to see how it plays out starting in  January

